/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*--------------------------- File parallel.dox ----------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_parallel */
/** \defgroup theory_parallel Theory of parallelism
 \ingroup area_parallel

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/parallel` realize, beyond the book, the
 parallel versions of a few algorithms of the library on the threads of one
 machine with shared memory: the patterns of the loops, of the reductions and
 of the prefix sums, the merge of sorted ranges and the sort, the products of
 matrices, the visits of a static graph, the LCP array of a text, the greedy
 algorithms on graphs by deterministic reservations and the shortest paths,
 and the runtime of the long-lived workers that talk by messages (their
 channels, the farm with feedback and the mailbox with the detection of the
 termination). Each runs on an executor, which is the only
 part that knows the threads, and gives the result of its sequential version.
 This page collects what the headers take for granted: the model of the cost
 and its notation (see \ref theory_parallel_notation), the executors and the
 way they schedule the calls (see \ref theory_parallel_exec), for each family
 of algorithms the property on which its correctness rests and the argument
 behind its bound (from \ref theory_parallel_patterns to
 \ref theory_parallel_sssp), and the runtime of the workers (from
 \ref theory_parallel_workers to \ref theory_parallel_quiescence). The
 pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on the page of its header, in the
 comment of its function, together with the paper that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_parallel_notation Definitions and notation

 A parallel computation is a graph of <em>tasks</em>, oriented and acyclic,
 with an edge from a task to each task that cannot start before it ends; the
 fork and join of two calls (`parallelInvoke()` of `Executor.h`) and the loop
 whose iterations are independent (`parallelFor()`) are the two ways in which
 the algorithms of the area build it. The <em>work</em> \f$ W \f$ is the time
 of all the tasks, i.e., the time of the computation on one thread, and the
 <em>depth</em> (or span) \f$ D \f$ is the time of a longest path of the
 graph, i.e., the time on infinitely many threads; their ratio
 \f$ W / D \f$ is the <em>parallelism</em>, the most threads that can be kept
 busy. An algorithm is <em>work-efficient</em> when its work is, up to a
 constant factor, the time of the best sequential algorithm: the ones of the
 area are, save the overheads stated in their headers, so that on one thread
 they lose only a constant.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ p \f$</td>
     <td>the threads of an executor, its <tt>concurrency()</tt>, the
         calling thread included where it works</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ W \f$, \f$ D \f$</td>
     <td>the work and the depth of a computation</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ T_p \f$</td>
     <td>the time on \f$ p \f$ threads, so that \f$ T_1 = W \f$ and
         \f$ T_\infty = D \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ g \f$, \f$ B \f$</td>
     <td>the grain, i.e., the least number of indices of a block of a loop
         (<tt>kGrain</tt> by default), and the number of blocks, at most
         <tt>kBlocks</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \oplus \f$</td>
     <td>an associative operation of a reduction or of a prefix sum, with
         the identity <tt>id</tt> or the initial value
         <tt>init</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ m \f$</td>
     <td>the items of the input, the nodes and the arcs of a graph</td></tr>
 </table>

 Two lower bounds hold for any schedule, since \f$ p \f$ threads do at most
 \f$ p \f$ units of work in a unit of time and the tasks of a path run one
 after the other:
 \f[
   T_p \ge W / p , \qquad T_p \ge D . \tag{1}
 \f]
 The <em>speedup</em> is \f$ T_1 / T_p \f$, at most \f$ p \f$ by (1), and it is
 <em>linear</em> when it is \f$ \Theta( p ) \f$, which needs a parallelism
 \f$ W / D \f$ larger than \f$ p \f$. The model counts the operations and not
 the moves of the memory, while several algorithms of the area (the products
 of a sparse matrix by a vector, the distribution of the sort) are bound by
 the bandwidth of the memory, which the threads of a socket share; their
 headers state the number of passes over the memory, on which their speedup
 depends more than on (1).

 \section theory_parallel_exec Executors and schedules

 An executor runs the calls \f$ f( 0 ) , \ldots , f( n - 1 ) \f$ on its
 threads and returns when all have returned (`bulk( n , f )`, the concept
 `Executor` of `Executor.h`), and may run two calls in parallel
 (`invoke( f , g )`); every parallel algorithm of the library takes one as its
 first argument, so that the same algorithm runs on any of the five.

 <table>
 <caption>The executors and the way they schedule the calls</caption>
 <tr><th>Executor</th><th>Threads</th><th><tt>bulk( n , f )</tt></th>
     <th><tt>invoke( f , g )</tt></th><th>A call nested in a call</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Sequential</tt></td><td>the caller</td>
     <td>the calls in order</td><td>\f$ f \f$, then \f$ g \f$</td>
     <td>in order</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ThreadPool</tt></td><td>\f$ p - 1 \f$ workers and the
     caller</td>
     <td>\f$ \min\{ p - 1 , n - 1 \} \f$ tokens on the deque of the caller,
         stolen by the others, each claiming calls by a fetch-and-add</td>
     <td>\f$ g \f$ pushed on the deque, \f$ f \f$ run, \f$ g \f$ taken
         back unless stolen</td><td>on all the threads, any depth</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>FastFlowExecutor</tt></td><td>\f$ p \f$ workers, the caller
     waiting</td>
     <td>a <tt>ParallelFor</tt> of FastFlow, chunks of 1</td>
     <td>by <tt>bulk()</tt></td><td>in order on its thread</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>OpenMPExecutor</tt></td><td>\f$ p \f$, the caller
     included</td>
     <td><tt>omp parallel for</tt>, dynamic chunks of 1</td>
     <td>by <tt>bulk()</tt> on 2 indices</td>
     <td>as the runtime nests a region, by default in order</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>TBBExecutor</tt></td><td>an arena of \f$ p \f$, the caller
     included</td>
     <td><tt>parallel_for</tt> of oneTBB, a task per index</td>
     <td><tt>parallel_invoke</tt></td><td>as tasks of the arena</td></tr>
 </table>

 <em>Lemma 1 (the greedy schedule).</em> A schedule that never leaves a thread
 idle while a task is ready runs in
 \f[
   T_p \le W / p + D , \tag{2}
 \f]
 within a factor of 2 of the best, by (1) (Graham, and Brent for the
 computations of known depth).

 Indeed, at each unit of time either all the \f$ p \f$ threads work, which
 can happen at most \f$ W / p \f$ times, or some thread is idle; in the second
 case every ready task runs, and the first task of each longest path among the
 tasks left is ready, so that the depth of what is left decreases by 1, which
 can happen at most \f$ D \f$ times. The patterns of `Executor.h` approach the
 greedy schedule from the side of the data: a loop is cut into \f$ B \f$
 blocks, which the threads claim one at a time from a counter, so that a
 thread is idle only when no block is left, and the time is
 \f$ T_p \le W / p + \max_b w_b \f$ for blocks of work \f$ w_b \f$. With
 blocks of equal work \f$ W / B \f$ this is \f$ ( W / p ) ( 1 + p / B ) \f$,
 and the cut into at most `kBlocks` blocks of at least `kGrain` indices
 trades this loss against the cost of a claim, a fetch-and-add on a shared
 line.

 The pool of ADS (`ThreadPool`) schedules by work stealing: each thread keeps
 its tasks on a deque of Chase and Lev, takes them from the bottom, and, when
 it has none, steals the oldest of a random other thread. With work stealing
 the expected time is \f$ W / p + O( D ) \f$ (Blumofe and Leiserson): a thief
 that fails is charged to the depth, since each round of \f$ p \f$ steals
 makes progress on the critical path with constant probability, and the
 thread that works is charged to the work; the expected number of steals is
 \f$ O( p D ) \f$, and since a steal moves the oldest task, which in a fork
 and join is the largest, the steals are few when the parallelism is large.
 The job of `bulk()` pushes \f$ \min\{ p - 1 , n - 1 \} \f$ tokens that the
 thieves take one at a time and that claim the calls from a shared counter,
 so that its calls are balanced as in the dynamic schedule and no task is
 allocated; `invoke()` waits for a stolen call by running other tasks (the
 leapfrogging of Wagner and Calder), so that a thread that waits still works
 and the bound holds for the nested calls too.

 The bounds above count the units of work, not where they run: a thread
 that the system moves to another processor finds there cold caches, and on
 a machine of several sockets the memory it touched first stays on the
 node of the old one. `Affinity.h` gives the processors that the process
 may use, as `taskset` or `numactl` left them, and pins the calling thread
 to the \f$ i \f$-th of them (modulo their number), so that the workers of
 a pool that pin themselves at their start fill those processors in order
 and keep them; the executors do not pin their threads by themselves,
 since two programs pinned to the same processors would slow each other
 down, and the choice is left to the caller.

 \section theory_parallel_patterns Loops, reductions and prefix sums

 The blocks of a loop (`blocksOf()` of `Executor.h`) depend only on the number
 of indices and on the grain: \f$ s = \max\{ g , \lceil n / \mathit{kBlocks}
 \rceil \} \f$ indices per block and \f$ \lceil n / s \rceil \f$ blocks.
 `parallelReduce()` reduces each block by a call `f( lo , hi )` into a partial
 result and combines the partial results by \f$ \oplus \f$ in the order of the
 blocks, so that its result is
 \f[
   \mathit{id} \oplus r_0 \oplus r_1 \oplus \cdots \oplus r_{B - 1} ,
   \qquad r_b = f( \mathit{lo}_b , \mathit{hi}_b ) , \tag{3}
 \f]
 whatever the executor and the number of its threads; (3) is the sum by blocks,
 which equals the sum from left to right when \f$ \oplus \f$ is associative,
 and is the same on every executor also when \f$ \oplus \f$ is associative
 only up to rounding, as the sum of floating point numbers is. The reduction
 has work \f$ O( n ) \f$ and depth \f$ O( n / B + B ) \f$, the blocks and then
 their combination.

 The prefix sums \f$ y_i = x_0 \oplus \cdots \oplus x_i \f$ (or up to
 \f$ x_{i-1} \f$, the exclusive ones) are the pattern of the counting sorts,
 of the compaction of a vector and of the distribution of the parallel sort.
 The scan of Blelloch reduces the blocks, scans the \f$ B \f$ partial results
 and then scans each block from its prefix, i.e., it reads the input twice and
 writes the output once, \f$ 3 n \f$ moves. `parallelInclusiveScan()` and
 `parallelExclusiveScan()` do it in one pass by the decoupled look-back of
 Merrill and Garland: the threads claim the tiles in increasing order, reduce
 each tile \f$ c \f$ into its aggregate \f$ a_c \f$, publish it, and add on
 the left the values published by the tiles before it until one has published
 its inclusive prefix \f$ P_{c'} \f$, so that
 \f[
   P_c = P_{c'} \oplus a_{c' + 1} \oplus \cdots \oplus a_{c - 1} \oplus a_c ;
   \tag{4}
 \f]
 then they publish \f$ P_c \f$ and scan the tile from the prefix before it,
 which is still in the cache: \f$ 2 n \f$ moves.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the look-back ends).</em> The scan by decoupled look-back ends on
 any executor, and its prefixes are those of the scan from left to right.

 In fact, a thread waits only for a tile before its own, and the tiles are
 claimed in increasing order: a tile before \f$ c \f$ is held by a thread that
 runs, since it claimed it before \f$ c \f$ was claimed, and the least tile
 not done waits for no tile, since all the tiles before it have published
 their prefix. Hence, by induction on the tiles, each tile is done, also when
 the threads of the executor are fewer than the tiles or when one runs all of
 them in order (`Sequential`). The prefixes are right by (4) and the
 associativity of \f$ \oplus \f$, since the tiles from \f$ c' + 1 \f$ to
 \f$ c - 1 \f$ added on the left are exactly those after the last prefix met.
 The look-back reads \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ tiles on average when the threads go at
 the same pace, since the tile before is then about to publish its prefix, and
 at most \f$ c \f$ tiles; an exception in a tile publishes it as failed, so
 that the tiles after it stop instead of waiting.

 \section theory_parallel_sort Merging and sorting

 The merge of \f$ k \f$ sorted ranges of \f$ N \f$ items in all
 (`multiwayMerge()` of `ParallelMerge.h`) is cut into \f$ B \f$ parts of the
 output, each merged by the sequential merge on one thread; it rests on the
 following.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the multisequence selection).</em> Order the items by the
 triples \f$ ( \mathit{item} , \mathit{range} , \mathit{position} ) \f$.
 For each rank \f$ r \le N \f$ there is a unique split \f$ s( r ) \f$, with
 \f[
   \sum_{i = 1}^{k} s_i( r ) = r , \tag{5}
 \f]
 such that the first \f$ s_i( r ) \f$ items of each range \f$ i \f$ are the
 \f$ r \f$ least items, and the merge writes them, in its order, before the
 others.

 Indeed, the order of the triples is total and, within a range, it is the
 order of the positions, since the range is sorted; hence the \f$ r \f$ least
 triples are a prefix of each range, and the order of the triples is the order
 of the stable merge, which on equal items takes the range of lower index
 first. The part \f$ q \f$ of the output is then the merge of the pieces
 \f$ [ s_i( r_q ) , s_i( r_{q+1} ) ) \f$ of the ranges, written from the rank
 \f$ r_q \f$, and the parts are independent. `multisequenceSplit()` finds
 \f$ s( r ) \f$ by narrowing a window on each range: the pivot is the middle
 of a window, chosen as the median of the middles weighted by the widths, and
 the binary searches that place it in the other windows tell whether it is
 among the \f$ r \f$ least. If it is, every window whose middle is not larger
 loses its left half, and these windows weigh at least half of the total width
 by the choice of the median; otherwise the same holds on the right. At least
 a quarter of the total width goes at each step, hence there are
 \f$ O( \log( k N ) ) \f$ steps, each of \f$ k \f$ binary searches and of a
 sort of \f$ k \f$ middles, i.e., \f$ O( k \log N \log( k N ) ) \f$
 comparisons per split, far fewer than the \f$ O( N \log k ) \f$ of the merge.
 With \f$ B = 4 p \f$ parts the merge takes \f$ O( ( N / p ) \log k ) \f$ time
 plus the splits, which run in parallel.

 The parallel sorts of `ParallelSort.h` (`sort( ex , A , less )` and
 `stableSort( ex , A , less )`) first look for the inputs that need no
 distribution: a reduction by blocks finds whether \f$ A \f$ is sorted or in
 decreasing order, and a second one whether it is made of a few runs per
 block, which are merged by the merge above. Otherwise they distribute the
 items into \f$ k \f$ buckets whose concatenation is sorted, as the samplesort
 does: \f$ k - 1 \f$ splitters taken from a sorted random sample of
 \f$ 16 k \f$ items (oversampling, so that every bucket holds
 \f$ O( n / k ) \f$ items with high probability), or the highest bits of the
 keys of the numbers in their natural order. The classification is a parallel
 loop on the blocks, each counting its items per bucket; a prefix sum of the
 counts in the order (bucket, block) gives each block the place of its items
 of each bucket, and a second loop moves them there, the blocks in their
 order, so that the distribution is stable. Last, the buckets are sorted by the
 sequential sort, claimed from the largest down (the schedule LPT of Graham,
 within \f$ 4 / 3 \f$ of the best), and a bucket larger than \f$ n / p \f$ is
 sorted by the parallel sort itself. With \f$ k \f$ buckets the time is
 \f$ O( n / p + k p ) \f$ for the classification and
 \f$ O( ( n / p ) \log( n / k ) ) \f$ for the buckets when they are balanced,
 and each item moves a constant number of times. The learned sorts of the same
 header (`learnedSort( ex , A )` and `learnedStableSort( ex , A )`) change the
 classifier only: on numbers of 32 or 64 bits, the bucket of a key is read from
 a model of the distribution function of a sample (see `LearnedSort.h`), a grid
 whose cell leaves a few comparisons with no branch instead of the
 \f$ \log_2 k \f$ dependent steps of the tree, and the sort falls back on the
 classifiers above when the sample shows a distribution that the model does
 not fit; the bound is the same, since the model keeps the buckets balanced
 with high probability by the same argument of the sample.

 \section theory_parallel_numeric Matrix products

 The product of a sparse matrix of \f$ m \f$ rows and \f$ \mathit{nnz} \f$
 nonzeros by a vector (`multiply()` of `ParallelNumeric.h`) costs, for the
 row \f$ i \f$, its nonzeros plus the store of its sum, i.e., the rows up to
 \f$ i \f$ cost \f$ i + \mathit{start}( i ) \f$, which grows with \f$ i \f$.
 Hence the cut of this quantity into \f$ B \f$ equal parts, each found by a
 binary search, balances the parts (the diagonals of the merge path of
 Merrill and Garland, applied to whole rows), and each row is summed on one
 thread in the order of the sequential product, so that the result is the same
 bit for bit; only a row longer than a part makes the parts unequal. The
 product moves \f$ 12 \mathit{nnz} + 8 ( m + n ) \f$ bytes for
 \f$ 2 \mathit{nnz} \f$ operations, i.e., it is bound by the bandwidth of the
 memory, and its speedup is at most the ratio of the bandwidth of the socket
 to that of one core. The product of dense matrices (`gemm()`) is instead bound
 by the operations, \f$ 2 m n k \f$, and runs the loops of the sequential
 product on blocks that fit the caches, the thread of each range of rows of
 \f$ C \f$ packing its own blocks of \f$ A \f$ and all of them sharing the
 packed panel of \f$ B \f$ (Smith et al.).

 \section theory_parallel_graph Visits of graphs and the LCP array

 The levels of a visit in breadth are
 \f[
   L_0 = \{ s \} , \qquad L_{\ell + 1} = \{ v \notin L_0 \cup \cdots \cup
   L_\ell : ( u , v ) \in E \text{ for some } u \in L_\ell \} , \tag{6}
 \f]
 and a node of \f$ L_\ell \f$ is at distance \f$ \ell \f$ from \f$ s \f$ (by
 Lemma 1, see \ref theory_graph_visits). `bfsDistances()` of `ParallelGraph.h`
 builds each level by (6) in either direction: from the top down, the nodes of
 \f$ L_\ell \f$ claim their neighbors not yet reached by a compare-and-swap
 of the distance, which succeeds for one claimant only and writes the same
 level whoever wins; from the bottom up, each node not yet reached looks for
 one neighbor in \f$ L_\ell \f$, and stops at the first. Both give the set
 (6), and hence the same distances; they differ in the arcs they read, the arcs
 out of the frontier \f$ m_f \f$ against those into the nodes not yet reached
 up to their first hit, at most \f$ m_u \f$. The visit goes from the bottom up
 when \f$ m_f > m_u / \alpha \f$, which happens on the few large levels of the
 graphs of small diameter, and back when the frontier is small again (Beamer,
 Asanovic and Patterson). The work is \f$ O( n + m ) \f$, each level costs a
 depth of \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ to gather its frontier, and the depth is
 therefore \f$ O( D \log n ) \f$ for diameter \f$ D \f$.

 The connected components (`connectedComponents()`) are those of a concurrent
 MFSET in which a merge hooks the larger of two roots under the smaller by a
 compare-and-swap, and tries again from the new roots if another thread moved
 first. The parent of a node is then never larger than the node, hence the
 parents form no cycle, the root of a set is its least node, and after the
 merges of all the arcs the sets are the components, whatever the order of the
 merges. The sampling of Afforest (Sutton, Ben-Nun and Barak) skips most of
 the merges: after the merges of the first two arcs of each node, a sample of
 nodes finds the largest set \f$ c \f$, and the nodes of \f$ c \f$ merge no
 more. On an unoriented graph this loses no arc between two components, since
 such an arc has an end out of \f$ c \f$, which merges it; the arcs within the
 giant component, usually most of them, are never read.

 The LCP array of a text \f$ T \f$ of \f$ n \f$ symbols and of its suffix
 array \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$ (`lcpArray()` of `ParallelText.h`) is read from the
 permuted array \f$ \mathit{PLCP}[ i ] = \mathrm{lcp}( i , \Phi[ i ] ) \f$,
 \f$ \Phi[ i ] \f$ the suffix before \f$ i \f$ in the order of
 \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$, which has
 \f[
   \mathit{PLCP}[ i ] \ge \mathit{PLCP}[ i - 1 ] - 1 . \tag{7}
 \f]
 In fact, if the suffixes \f$ i - 1 \f$ and \f$ \Phi[ i - 1 ] \f$ share a
 prefix of \f$ h > 0 \f$ symbols, the suffix \f$ \Phi[ i - 1 ] + 1 \f$ comes
 before \f$ i \f$ and shares \f$ h - 1 \f$ symbols with it, and so does every
 suffix between them in the order, \f$ \Phi[ i ] \f$ included. By (7) a scan
 of \f$ i \f$ in the order of the text resumes each comparison at
 \f$ h - 1 \f$, and since \f$ h \f$ grows by at most \f$ n \f$ in all and
 decreases by 1 per suffix, it makes \f$ O( n ) \f$ comparisons (Kasai et
 al.). The parallel version cuts the text into \f$ 4 p \f$ parts, each
 scanned from \f$ h = 0 \f$: (7) is then not used across the borders, which
 costs at most \f$ \max_i \mathit{PLCP}[ i ] \f$ more per part and leaves the
 result the same.

 \section theory_parallel_reserve Deterministic reservations, greedy algorithms

 A greedy algorithm scans its items in a fixed order and decides each of them
 on the state that the earlier ones left, e.g., the algorithm of Kruskal keeps
 an edge if no earlier edge joined its ends (see \ref theory_graph_mst). The
 <em>deterministic reservations</em> of Blelloch, Fineman, Gibbons and Shun
 (`speculativeFor()` of `SpeculativeFor.h`) run such a loop of iterations
 \f$ s , s + 1 , \ldots , e - 1 \f$ on the threads of an executor, each
 iteration \f$ i \f$ split into two steps: \f$ \mathrm{reserve}( i ) \f$
 reads the state and either finds that \f$ i \f$ has nothing to do or writes
 \f$ i \f$ into a <em>reservation</em> of each place of the state that it
 would change, by a priority write that keeps the least index there; and
 \f$ \mathrm{commit}( i ) \f$ changes the state if \f$ i \f$ holds all its
 reservations, and otherwise leaves \f$ i \f$ to the next round. A round runs
 the reserves of a prefix of \f$ r \f$ iterations not yet done, in parallel,
 then their commits in parallel, and keeps the failed ones, in their order,
 at the head of the next prefix.

 <em>Lemma 4 (determinism of the reservations).</em> If each
 \f$ \mathrm{reserve}( i ) \f$ reserves every place that
 \f$ \mathrm{commit}( i ) \f$ may change, and reads only places that the
 iterations before \f$ i \f$ may change, then the least iteration of each
 round commits, and the state at the end is the one of the sequential loop,
 whatever the executor and the number of its threads.

 Indeed, the least iteration of a round wins every priority write, hence it
 holds all its reservations and commits, and each round makes progress. An
 iteration \f$ i \f$ commits only if no earlier iteration of its round wants
 one of its places, and every earlier iteration of an earlier round is done;
 so the state that \f$ i \f$ read in its reserve is the one that the
 sequential loop leaves before \f$ i \f$ on the places that \f$ i \f$ reads,
 and its commit writes what the sequential loop would write. The failures
 depend only on the iterations and on their order, not on the timing of the
 threads; hence the sequence of the rounds, and the size \f$ r \f$ of their
 prefixes, which halves when more than a fifth of a round fails and doubles
 when less than a tenth does, are themselves deterministic.

 With \f$ W \f$ the work of the sequential loop and \f$ R \f$ that of the
 iterations tried again, the work is \f$ O( W + R ) \f$ and the depth is that
 of the rounds, each of depth \f$ O( \log r ) \f$ for the pack of the
 failures. For the maximal independent set and the maximal matching in a
 random order the iterations that wait for each other form chains of length
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ with high probability (Blelloch, Fineman and Shun gave
 \f$ O( \log^2 n ) \f$, and Fischer and Noever the tight bound), so that few
 iterations fail and the rounds are few.

 `ParallelGreedy.h` builds on the reservations the greedy algorithms of the
 table below, each with the result of its sequential version on the same
 order. In `parallelKruskal()` the edge \f$ i \f$, in the order of
 \f$ ( w( e ) , e ) \f$, reserves the roots of its ends in a union-find if
 they differ, and its commit hangs the root that it holds under the other;
 the finds run while no link happens, so that the halving of their paths
 writes values that a concurrent find reads either way. `filterKruskal()`
 (Osipov, Sanders and Singler) splits the edges by a pivot \f$ p \f$ into the
 light ones \f$ e < p \f$ and the heavy ones, solves the light ones first,
 and then drops, by a parallel filter, the heavy edges whose ends the forest
 already joins, before their recursion; a heavy edge dropped would be refused
 by Kruskal too, and the light edges come first in the order, so that the
 forest is the same, while the edges dropped are never sorted. In
 `maximalIndependentSet()` the reserve of a node looks at its neighbors of
 earlier places, one in the set leaving it out and one undecided making it
 wait, and in `maximalMatching()` an edge reserves its two ends if both are
 free and takes them if it holds both.

 <table>
 <caption>The greedy algorithms of <tt>ParallelGreedy.h</tt>, on
 \f$ n \f$ nodes and \f$ m \f$ edges</caption>
 <tr><th>Function</th><th>Order</th><th>Work</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>parallelKruskal()</tt></td><td>\f$ ( w( e ) , e ) \f$, by
     the parallel sort</td><td>\f$ O( m \log m ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>filterKruskal()</tt></td><td>as above, sorting only the edges
     not filtered</td><td>\f$ O( m + n \log n \log( m / n ) ) \f$ expected on
     random weights</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>spanningForest()</tt></td><td>the order of the edges</td>
     <td>\f$ O( m \, \alpha( m , n ) ) \f$ plus the retries</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>maximalIndependentSet()</tt></td><td>a permutation
     \f$ \pi \f$ of the nodes</td><td>\f$ O( n + m ) \f$ expected on a
     random \f$ \pi \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>maximalMatching()</tt></td><td>a permutation \f$ \pi \f$ of
     the edges</td><td>\f$ O( n + m ) \f$ expected on a random
     \f$ \pi \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_parallel_sssp Shortest paths by steps

 The shortest paths from \f$ s \f$ with costs that are not negative (see
 \ref theory_graph_sp) are the least solution of
 \f[
   d( s ) = 0 , \qquad d( u ) = \min\bigl\{ d( u ) , \min_{( v , u ) \in E}
   d( v ) \oplus w( v , u ) \bigr\} , \tag{8}
 \f]
 where \f$ \oplus \f$ is the sum of the computer, which for the integers is
 the sum and for the floating points is the sum rounded, in both cases
 monotone, \f$ a \le b \Rightarrow a \oplus w \le b \oplus w \f$, and not
 decreasing, \f$ a \oplus w \ge a \f$ for \f$ w \ge 0 \f$.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the label-correcting steps).</em> Start from \f$ d( s ) = 0 \f$
 and \f$ d( v ) = \infty \f$ elsewhere, with \f$ s \f$ active, and repeat, in
 any order and with any number of threads, steps that take some active nodes,
 make them not active, and relax their arcs, each relaxation
 \f$ d( u ) \gets \min\{ d( u ) , d( v ) \oplus w( v , u ) \} \f$ atomic and
 making \f$ u \f$ active (adding it to a bucket, or setting its flag) if it
 lowers \f$ d( u ) \f$. When no node is active,
 \f$ d \f$ is the least solution of (8), the distances of the algorithm of
 Dijkstra.

 Indeed, each \f$ d( u ) \f$ is at every moment the value of a path from
 \f$ s \f$ summed by \f$ \oplus \f$ from \f$ s \f$, and hence at least the
 least solution, which is the least such value; and when no node is active,
 every arc was relaxed after the last decrease of its tail, which by the
 monotonicity left \f$ d( u ) \le d( v ) \oplus w( v , u ) \f$ on it, so that
 \f$ d \f$ solves (8), and being at least the least solution, it is the
 least. The algorithm of Dijkstra computes the same solution, since by the
 two properties of \f$ \oplus \f$ its order of extraction never lowers the
 distance of a node already extracted. A node made active while it is being
 relaxed is relaxed again: the flag that says whether it is active is cleared
 before its distance is read, and a relaxation that lowers the distance reads
 the flag after it, both sequentially consistent, so that one of the two sees
 the other.

 The algorithms differ in the nodes that each step takes. The algorithm of
 Dijkstra takes one, the least, and relaxes each arc once; that of Bellman,
 Ford and Moore by rounds takes all the active nodes, \f$ O( n ) \f$ steps
 of large frontiers, and relaxes an arc as many times as its tail improves.
 The \f$ \Delta \f$-stepping of Meyer and Sanders (`deltaStepping()` of
 `ParallelShortestPaths.h`) takes the active nodes in the bucket
 \f$ \lfloor d / \Delta \rfloor \f$ of the least active distance \f$ \mu \f$,
 again while the bucket is not empty: a node of a later bucket has a
 distance at least the end of the bucket, and since the costs are not
 negative it lowers no distance within it, so that the nodes of the bucket
 are at their final distances when it is empty, and the extra work is that
 of the nodes of the bucket relaxed before they reached them. For costs
 independent and uniform in \f$ [ 0 , 1 ] \f$ and \f$ \Delta \f$ of the
 order of the inverse of the largest degree \f$ d \f$, Meyer and Sanders
 bound the expected work by \f$ O( n + m + d L ) \f$ and the depth by
 \f$ O( d L \log n + \log^2 n ) \f$, \f$ L \f$ the largest distance.

 The steps of `deltaStepping()` are those of the GAP benchmark suite
 (Beamer, Asanović and Patterson; the code of Beamer and Zhang): each
 thread appends the nodes that it improves to buckets of its own, with no
 flag, so that a node is in a bucket once per improvement and is skipped,
 when its bucket comes, if its distance has meanwhile fallen below it
 (it was then relaxed in an earlier bucket, by Lemma 5 at its last
 distance); a step relaxes the frontier of the bucket \f$ c \f$, then each
 thread its own bucket \f$ c \f$ while it is small, and the threads meet at
 a barrier, vote the next bucket, copy their parts of it into the
 frontier, each at the places that the counts of the others give it, and
 meet at a second barrier. Two barriers a step, against the seven passes
 of a step that gathers its frontier by blocks, is what makes the steps of
 a graph of large diameter cheap, where most steps hold a few hundred
 nodes. The threads are those of the calls of one `bulk()` of the
 executor, which run the whole computation; a call that starts late joins
 it at a barrier, which counts the threads that joined at its release.
 Hence a barrier waits only for calls that are running, each of which
 reaches it, and the computation ends on any executor, also on one that
 runs the calls one after the other, where the first call does all the
 work and the others find it over. The buckets of a thread are a window
 of 1024 buckets from a base and an overflow past it, which the threads
 spread over the next window when the least bucket voted is past the
 current one (the open buckets of Julienne, of Dhulipala, Blelloch and
 Shun), so that the memory does not grow with \f$ L / \Delta \f$. With
 \f$ \Delta \f$ above the least costs a node improves many times within
 its bucket and is put back into it each time, and its entries would
 relax its arcs again at a distance already relaxed: from the step where
 the entries put back into the bucket of the step outnumber a quarter of
 those relaxed, each node keeps the distance at which it was last
 relaxed, and an entry that finds its node at that distance relaxes
 nothing. This is safe whatever the threads, since the arcs of a node at
 a distance are relaxed by whoever stored it, before the barrier that
 ends the step, and it costs a read and a write per entry, which is why
 it starts only when the entries put back show that it pays.

 The \f$ \rho \f$-stepping of Dong, Gu, Sun and Zhang (`rhoStepping()`) takes
 the \f$ \rho \f$ active nodes of least distance, or all of them when they
 are no more than \f$ \rho \f$, with the threshold estimated on a sample of
 1024 of them, so that the steps have a frontier of the same size whatever
 the scale of the costs; on a graph whose frontier
 stays below \f$ \rho \f$, e.g., a grid or a road network, it is the
 algorithm of Bellman, Ford and Moore by rounds, and a smaller \f$ \rho \f$
 is needed there. Each of its steps costs \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ depth for
 the gathering of its frontier, so that the steps should be few; the nodes
 that
 a block of arcs makes active within the threshold of its step are relaxed
 by the same block at once, up to 1024 of them, as in the fusion of the
 buckets of Zhang et al., which saves the steps of the chains of light arcs.

 \section theory_parallel_workers Long-lived workers and their locks

 The calls of `bulk( n , f )` are tasks: an executor may run several of them
 on one thread, one after the other, and a call starts only when a thread is
 free to take it, so that a call that waits for another one (at a barrier,
 or for a message) may wait forever for a call that no thread will start
 before it returns. A computation of <em>workers</em>, each a loop that
 lives as long as the computation and talks to the others (the workers of a
 parallel search, each with its own queue of nodes and its inbox, or the
 master and the workers of a farm), needs instead that its \f$ p \f$ calls
 run at the same time on \f$ p \f$ distinct threads; `run( p , f )` of the
 executors with workers (the concept `WorkerExecutor` of `Executor.h`) gives
 this, together with `lock_type`, the mutex that the executor prefers for the
 short sections that the workers share. Every `lock_type` is Lockable in the
 sense of the standard, i.e., it has `try_lock()` besides `lock()` and
 `unlock()`, which takes the lock only if it is free and never waits: a
 worker that finds a shared section busy (the update of a common bound, the
 publication of a part of its work) may then skip it and come back later,
 instead of queueing behind the holder.

 <table>
 <caption>How each executor runs the \f$ p \f$ calls of <tt>run( p , f )
 </tt>, and its lock</caption>
 <tr><th>Executor</th><th>The calls</th><th>Why they run together</th>
     <th><tt>lock_type</tt></th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Sequential</tt></td><td>\f$ f( 0 ) \f$ on the caller,
     \f$ p \le 1 \f$</td><td>one worker</td>
     <td><tt>std::mutex</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ThreadPool</tt></td><td>\f$ f( 0 ) \f$ on the caller, the
     others claimed one each under a mutex by the workers idle in their
     loop</td><td>a thread that waits inside a call never claims one</td>
     <td><tt>std::mutex</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>OpenMPExecutor</tt></td><td>a region <tt>omp parallel</tt> of
     \f$ p \f$ threads, \f$ f( i ) \f$ on its thread \f$ i \f$</td>
     <td>the team has \f$ p \f$ threads, or nothing runs and
     <tt>run()</tt> throws</td><td><tt>OpenMPLock</tt>, i.e.,
     <tt>omp_lock_t</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>TBBExecutor</tt></td><td>a <tt>parallel_for</tt> of the arena,
     a task per call, each call inside <tt>isolate()</tt></td>
     <td>a thread that waits inside a call takes only the tasks of the jobs
     nested in it</td><td><tt>tbb::spin_mutex</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>FastFlowExecutor</tt></td><td>a <tt>parallel_for</tt> of its
     <tt>ParallelFor</tt> on \f$ p \f$ workers, chunks of 1</td>
     <td>each worker is given its own index first</td>
     <td><tt>FastFlowLock</tt>, i.e., <tt>ff::lock_t</tt></td></tr>
 </table>

 A worker that binds itself to a processor (to keep its caches, and its
 memory on its node) must not bind the thread of the caller, which goes on
 after the run with the placement the program gave it; `isRunCaller()` of
 `Executor.h` tells a call whether it runs on that thread. The caller makes
 \f$ f( 0 ) \f$ on `Sequential`, `ThreadPool` and `OpenMPExecutor` (the
 thread 0 of a region of OpenMP is the thread that opens it), none of the
 calls on `FastFlowExecutor` with \f$ p > 1 \f$, where it only waits, and
 on `TBBExecutor` any one of them or none, since it joins the arena as one
 more thread; since the calls run at the same time on distinct threads, the
 caller holds at most one of them, and the mark is a variable of the
 thread set for the time of the run, so that a worker that asks costs a
 load.

 <em>Lemma 6 (the calls of a run meet).</em> Let a pool of `ThreadPool` have
 \f$ p - 1 \f$ workers idle in their loop besides the thread that calls
 `run( p , f )`. Then the \f$ p \f$ calls run at the same time, each on its
 own thread, whatever the calls do, nested jobs included.

 Indeed, the caller makes \f$ f( 0 ) \f$, and the other calls are claimed
 one at a time from a counter under a mutex, only by a worker that is in its
 loop, i.e., that holds no call of any job; a worker that has claimed a call
 makes it and goes back to its loop only after it has returned, so that no
 thread holds two calls of the run. A thread that waits inside a call, for a
 job nested in it or for the end of the run, steals and runs only tasks,
 never a call of the run: were it to run one, it would hold it below its own
 call, which might wait for it forever. Hence the \f$ p - 1 \f$ idle workers
 claim the \f$ p - 1 \f$ calls, one each, as soon as they wake (the run wakes
 the sleeping ones through the event count of the pool), and all the calls
 run together. On the other executors the same property rests on the
 runtime: the team of a region of OpenMP has its \f$ p \f$ threads from the
 start; a thread of oneTBB inside `isolate()` takes only the tasks spawned
 within it, and the arena has \f$ p \f$ threads when the threads of oneTBB
 are not fewer, i.e., for \f$ p \f$ not larger than the hardware threads;
 the `ParallelFor` of FastFlow cuts \f$ [ 0 , p ) \f$ into \f$ p \f$ chunks of
 1, one per worker, and a worker looks at the chunks of the others only
 after its own, so that a worker takes the call of another only when its own
 has returned, which leaves the calls that wait for each other on distinct
 workers.

 \section theory_parallel_channels Channels between workers

 Each of \f$ p \f$ workers has an <em>inbox</em>, into which the others
 push batches of messages and which only its owner drains; the messages of
 a sender to a receiver come out in the order in which they were pushed,
 while those of different senders interleave in any order. A batch of
 \f$ k \f$ messages is pushed by one operation of the channel, so that the
 cost of the synchronization (an atomic operation, a lock, a line of cache
 that moves between two cores) is paid once per batch and not per message;
 the four families of `Channels.h` differ in what they pay and when.

 <table>
 <caption>The families of channels: the cost of a push of a batch of
 \f$ k \f$ messages, of a drain of \f$ k \f$ messages, whether a push may
 refuse messages, and the memory for \f$ p \f$ workers</caption>
 <tr><th>Family</th><th>Push</th><th>Drain</th><th>Bounded</th>
     <th>Memory</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>LockedChannels</tt></td><td>a lock, \f$ k \f$ copies, a store
     of the size</td><td>a load of the size; if not 0, a lock and a swap of
     two vectors, then \f$ k \f$ calls</td><td>no</td>
     <td>the messages</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>InboxChannels</tt></td><td>a vector of \f$ k \f$ copies, a
     cell, one compare-and-swap</td><td>one exchange, the list of the
     \f$ b \f$ batches reversed, \f$ k \f$ calls</td><td>no</td>
     <td>the messages, a cell and a vector per batch</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>LaneChannels</tt></td><td>\f$ k \f$ stores with a release,
     no read-modify-write</td><td>the \f$ p \f$ lanes in turn, a read of
     the count per block, \f$ k \f$ calls</td><td>no</td>
     <td>\f$ p^2 \f$ lanes of blocks</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MeshChannels</tt></td><td>\f$ k \f$ copies, one store of a
     tail</td><td>the \f$ p \f$ queues of the worker, a load of a tail and a
     store of a head each, \f$ k \f$ calls</td><td>yes, \f$ c \f$ per
     pair</td><td>\f$ p^2 c \f$ places</td></tr>
 </table>

 The inboxes under a lock are the baseline that every executor can build
 with its own lock, and the only family whose memory does not depend on the
 pairs of workers; a sender and the owner meet on the lock, and senders meet
 among themselves. The stack of Treiber of `InboxChannels` makes the push of
 a batch lock-free, one compare-and-swap whatever \f$ k \f$, and the drain
 one exchange whatever the number of senders, at the price of an allocation
 per batch. The lanes of `LaneChannels` and the mesh of `MeshChannels` give
 each ordered pair of workers a queue of its own, so that two senders never
 touch the same line and a push needs no read-modify-write, the queues of
 the farms and of the all-to-all of FastFlow; a drain then scans the
 \f$ p \f$ queues of its worker, \f$ O( p ) \f$ loads even when they are
 empty, and the memory grows as \f$ p^2 \f$, which on the threads of one
 machine (tens of workers) is cheap. The mesh is bounded: a push that finds
 its queue full pushes what fits and returns the number, and the mailbox
 keeps the rest in its outbox (see \ref theory_parallel_quiescence), so
 that a worker is never blocked by a receiver that does not drain.
 On the benchmark (`bench/runtime.cpp`, 4 and 8 workers on the pool, on
 OpenMP and on FastFlow, one per core, messages of 8 bytes) the mesh is the
 fastest family whenever the workers exchange batches: with batches of 64
 messages it takes 1.4 to 2.5 times less per message than the inboxes under
 a lock (whichever lock), 3 to 4.5 times less than the stacks of batches and
 the lanes, and 1.9 to 2.7 times less than the mesh of the queues of FastFlow,
 which pass a pointer per message with a store of a flag each; with batches
 of 1 message it takes 2 to 5 times less than the inboxes under a lock,
 while the mesh of FastFlow is then within 0.7 to 1.8 times of it. A round
 trip of one message between two workers costs the mesh 1.1 to 1.7 times
 less than the inboxes under a lock, and the lanes pay there the wait of
 their consumer for a batch; the exploration of a tree spread over the
 workers, whose work per message dominates, is a tie among the families
 within the noise of the machine, the lanes and the stacks last. Hence
 `DefaultChannels` is the mesh on every executor, the inboxes under the lock
 of the executor stay the portable choice whose memory does not grow with
 \f$ p^2 \f$, and the lanes and the stacks of batches remain for the
 senders that cannot bound what they push.

 \section theory_parallel_farm The farm with feedback

 A farm with feedback has a <em>master</em>, which emits tasks, and \f$ w \f$
 workers, which turn each task into a result that goes back to the master;
 the master may emit more tasks from each result, and decides when the farm
 ends. It is the master and workers of FastFlow (a farm whose emitter
 receives the results of its workers on the channel of feedback), and
 `feedbackFarm()` of `Farm.h` runs it on a farm of FastFlow on
 `FastFlowExecutor`, and on `run( w + 1 , f )` of the other executors, the
 master being the call 0. The tasks go to the workers on demand, i.e., a
 task waits in a queue of many consumers [see `MPMCQueue.h`] until a worker
 is free, which balances tasks of different costs; the results come back on
 a queue with a lane per worker [see `MPSCQueue.h`].

 The workers have the indices \f$ 0 , \ldots , w - 1 \f$ and the master
 the index \f$ w \f$, which `work( t , i )` and `start( emit , w )` receive
 when they take them, so that each may keep a state of its own (an
 evaluator, a buffer, the processor to which it binds itself) in a vector of
 \f$ w + 1 \f$ places without a lock. An index stays on one thread for the
 whole farm, and distinct indices on distinct threads: on FastFlow the
 master is the emitter and the worker \f$ i \f$ the \f$ i \f$-th node of the
 farm, each a thread of FastFlow; on the other executors the master is the
 call 0 of `run( w + 1 , f )` and the worker \f$ i \f$ the call
 \f$ i + 1 \f$, which Lemma 6 keeps on distinct threads for the whole run.

 <table>
 <caption>The thread of each index of the farm</caption>
 <tr><th>Executor</th><th>Master, index \f$ w \f$</th>
     <th>Worker \f$ i \f$</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>FastFlowExecutor</tt></td><td>the emitter, a thread of
     FastFlow</td><td>the node \f$ i \f$, a thread of FastFlow</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ThreadPool</tt>, <tt>OpenMPExecutor</tt></td><td>the
     caller</td><td>the call \f$ i + 1 \f$ of the run, a thread of the
     executor</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>TBBExecutor</tt></td><td colspan="2">the calls \f$ 0 \f$ and
     \f$ i + 1 \f$ of the run, on threads of the arena, the caller among
     them or not</td></tr>
 <tr><td>any, \f$ w = 0 \f$</td><td colspan="2">the caller, which is the
     master and the worker 0</td></tr>
 </table>

 <em>Lemma 7 (the farm ends).</em> If `work()` returns on every task and the
 master emits finitely many tasks, the farm ends, and when it ends because no
 task is left the master has seen the result of every task it emitted.

 Indeed, the master counts in \f$ m \f$ the tasks emitted and not yet back:
 it adds 1 at each emission and subtracts 1 at each result, and a worker
 that takes a task always sends back a result (empty if the farm was stopped
 or the work threw), so that \f$ m \f$ is the number of the tasks in the
 queue, in the hands of a worker, or of their results in the queue back.
 While \f$ m > 0 \f$ some task or result exists, and since the free workers
 take the tasks and `work()` returns, the master receives a result in
 finite time; it emits finitely many tasks, so that \f$ m \f$ reaches 0, and
 then nothing is left to receive. When the master stops the farm instead,
 the workers end after the task they hold, whose result is dropped.

 \section theory_parallel_quiescence The detection of the termination

 In a computation by messages a worker is <em>active</em> while it has work,
 and <em>passive</em> otherwise; a passive worker becomes active only by
 receiving a message, and only an active worker sends. The computation has
 <em>terminated</em> when every worker is passive and no message is on its
 way, a state that lasts forever once reached (no worker can become active
 again), but that no worker sees by itself: a worker that has no work does
 not know whether a message for it is still on its way, or whether another
 worker is about to send one. The detection of the termination is the
 problem of Dijkstra and Scholten, who solved it for a computation that
 starts from one process with an acknowledgment of every message and a tree
 of the active processes, and of Mattern, who counted the messages sent and
 received with waves that read the counters of all the processes (see
 \ref theory_distributed_messages for the processes of MPI).

 In shared memory the counters can be one: the mailbox of `Mailbox.h` keeps
 a counter \f$ C \f$ of the activities and of the messages, which starts at
 \f$ p \f$ (every worker active). A worker adds \f$ k \f$ to \f$ C \f$
 before it pushes a batch of \f$ k \f$ messages (and subtracts the messages
 that a bounded channel refused); a passive worker that drains messages adds
 1 before it handles the first of them, and becomes active; a worker
 subtracts the \f$ k \f$ messages it drained once it has handled them; a
 worker that has no work and has pushed all its outboxes becomes passive and
 subtracts 1. The messages still in an outbox are not counted, but their
 sender is active, and it becomes passive only after it has pushed them.

 <em>Lemma 8 (the deficit counter).</em> At any time
 \f[
   C \ge A + M , \tag{10}
 \f]
 \f$ A \f$ the number of the active workers and \f$ M \f$ that of the
 messages pushed and not yet handled; and \f$ C = 0 \f$ if and only if the
 computation has terminated and every worker has seen it, i.e., has
 subtracted its activity.

 Indeed, every change of \f$ A + M \f$ is preceded by the change of \f$ C \f$
 that covers it: a batch is added before it is pushed, an activity is added
 before the messages that bring it are handled, and the messages and the
 activities are subtracted only after they have ended; the operations on
 \f$ C \f$ are atomic, so that they follow each other in one order, and
 (10) holds after each of them. If \f$ C = 0 \f$, (10) gives \f$ A = 0 \f$
 and \f$ M = 0 \f$, and no outbox holds a message, since its owner would be
 active: the computation has terminated, and since every increase of
 \f$ C \f$ is made by an active worker or for a message, \f$ C \f$ stays at
 0. Conversely, once the computation has terminated every message has been
 handled and subtracted, and each worker, finding no work, pushes its empty
 outboxes and subtracts its activity, which brings \f$ C \f$ to 0. A worker
 that sees \f$ C = 0 \f$ therefore knows that the computation is over, and
 a passive worker learns it by a load of \f$ C \f$; the detection costs one
 atomic addition per batch at its sender, one per drain at its receiver, and
 one per change of activity, so that the counter, which all the workers
 share, is touched once per batch and not per message. It is the deficit
 counting of Dijkstra and Scholten with the deficits of all the workers
 summed in one place, which the shared memory allows; across processes the
 sums need the waves of \ref theory_distributed_messages. The recovery of
 credits of Mattern, which splits a unit of weight among the messages and
 detects the end when the weight is back whole, also needs no wave, but
 pays an arithmetic of fractions per message where the counter pays one
 addition per batch.

 No operation of the mailbox waits for another worker. A bounded family
 (the mesh) may take only part of a batch, when the queue of the pair is
 full: the rest stays in the outbox, counted by nobody, while its owner
 stays active; `send()` tries it again only when a batch more has come for
 that destination, so that a full queue costs the sender one push, i.e.,
 two atomic additions on \f$ C \f$, per batch and not per message, and
 `flush()` tries every outbox and returns false while one is not empty.

 <em>Lemma 9 (the batches retained).</em> Let each worker whose
 `quiescent()` returns false poll its inbox before it calls `quiescent()`
 again. Then every message retained in an outbox is pushed in finite time,
 and Lemma 8 holds as it is.

 Indeed, a worker with a message in an outbox fails its `flush()`, stays
 active and holds its activity in \f$ C \f$, so that (10) and the argument
 of Lemma 8 do not change: the retained messages are not in \f$ M \f$, but
 their sender is in \f$ A \f$. A queue is full only of messages pushed and
 not yet drained, and its receiver drains all its queues at each poll; since
 every worker polls between two calls of `quiescent()`, each full queue is
 emptied in finite time, and the next `flush()` of the sender pushes into
 it. A cycle of workers each waiting to push into the queue of the next
 one cannot block, since none of them waits: each polls, which empties the
 queue that its predecessor waits for.

 A computation may also end before its termination, when one of its
 workers, or another thread, knows that the rest of the work is useless:
 a limit on the work done, or a bound that closes the search. `stop()` sets
 a flag that every operation reads: from then on `quiescent()` and
 `terminated()` return true, `poll()` drains nothing, `send()` only appends
 to its outbox and `flush()` pushes nothing. The messages on their way are
 not lost: once the workers have returned from `run()`, `leftovers()` hands
 to a function those of the outboxes and those of the inboxes, which a
 search puts back into its frontier, where they bound the optimum of what
 was not explored.

 <em>Lemma 10 (each message once).</em> Whatever the moment of `stop()`,
 also during the sends and the polls of the workers, each message given to
 `send()` is handed to a function exactly once, by a `poll()` before the
 stop or by `leftovers()` after the run.

 Indeed, a message is at any time in exactly one of three places: an
 outbox, which only its sender touches; a channel, between a push and a
 drain; or handed to a function. It moves from the first to the second only
 by a push of its sender, which copies it into the channel and removes it
 from the outbox in the same operation of that thread, and from the second
 to the third only by a drain of its receiver, which moves it out of the
 queue and calls the function on it; a push or a drain started before the
 flag is set runs to its end, and none starts after the flag is seen. Hence
 when the workers have returned no message is moving, each is in an outbox,
 in a channel or already handed, and `leftovers()`, which empties the
 outboxes and drains the channels on the thread that called `run()` (which
 the end of the run orders after every operation of the workers), hands
 each of the first two kinds once.

